From: Akisaira <akisaira@outlook.com>
Subject: [PATCH] PM: sleep: disarm wake IRQs before resuming device IRQs

dpm_resume_noirq() enables the device IRQs again and only then disarms
the wake IRQs. A dedicated wake IRQ that fired during the sleep is
re-enabled by resume_device_irqs(), IRQD_WAKEUP_ARMED is already cleared,
so the core runs its handler. handle_threaded_wake_irq() still sees the
wakeup set, reports a wakeup event and returns. If the line is level
triggered and still asserted, the IRQ fires again as soon as the oneshot
thread unmasks it, until device_wakeup_disarm_wake_irqs() disables it.

The PCIe WAKE# IRQ of 071e245ab749 ("PCI: Add support for PCIe WAKE#
interrupt") is such an IRQ, and the endpoint can still hold WAKE# at that
point. On a Huawei MateBook E Go (SC8280XP, WCN6855 Wi-Fi), in 20 sleeps
of which 19 ended on WAKE#, 6 resumes fired it 5 to 26,358 times over up
to 244 ms, the IRQ thread delaying the disarm on its CPU.

Disarm the wake IRQs before resume_device_irqs(). A dedicated wake IRQ
that the disarm disables is then still disabled when resume_irq() runs,
and stays disabled. Plain wake IRQs only lose the wake setting earlier:
resume_irq() restores an IRQ that suspend enabled for wakeup
(IRQCHIP_ENABLE_WAKEUP_ON_SUSPEND) from IRQD_IRQ_ENABLED_ON_SUSPEND,
which the disarm does not touch.

Side effects:
- The handler no longer runs for such a wakeup, so the wakeup is not
  counted on the device's wakeup source. pm_system_irq_wakeup() still
  records the IRQ.
- An edge triggered dedicated wake IRQ that fired during the sleep used
  to be replayed during resume. Now it stays pending until the IRQ is
  enabled next, at the next runtime suspend or system suspend, where the
  replay can report a wakeup and abort that suspend.

Not covered: a dedicated wake IRQ left enabled for runtime PM
(WAKE_IRQ_DEDICATED_ENABLED). The disarm doesn't disable it, so a held
level line keeps firing as before, and its handler now finds the wakeup
cleared and fails to resume the device while runtime PM is still
disabled ("wake IRQ with no resume: -13").

Assisted-by: Claude:claude-opus-5-5
Origin: local
Upstream-Status: Pending
---
 drivers/base/power/main.c | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/drivers/base/power/main.c b/drivers/base/power/main.c
index 4ed2e9b7e..106ade94c 100644
--- a/drivers/base/power/main.c
+++ b/drivers/base/power/main.c
@@ -938,8 +938,13 @@ void dpm_resume_noirq(pm_message_t state)
 {
 	dpm_noirq_resume_devices(state);
 
-	resume_device_irqs();
+	/*
+	 * Disarm first: a dedicated wake IRQ that is still asserted would
+	 * fire again as soon as resume_device_irqs() enables it, and keep
+	 * firing until the disarm disables it.
+	 */
 	device_wakeup_disarm_wake_irqs();
+	resume_device_irqs();
 }
 
 static void async_resume_early(void *data, async_cookie_t cookie);
